## 📝 Quick Reference

### CASE Syntax

```sql
SELECT
    column,
    CASE
        WHEN condition1 THEN 'result1'
        WHEN condition2 THEN 'result2'
        ELSE                 'default'
    END AS alias_name
FROM table;
```

### CASE Rules

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Evaluates top to bottom | First match wins — order your conditions carefully |
| Always add ELSE | Without it — unmatched rows return NULL |
| Always end with END | Required syntax |
| Give it an alias | Without AS alias — column has no name |

---

### CTE Syntax

```sql
-- Single CTE
WITH cte_name AS (
    SELECT ...
)
SELECT ... FROM cte_name;

-- Multiple CTEs
WITH cte1 AS (
    SELECT ...
),
cte2 AS (
    SELECT ... FROM cte1   -- can reference previous CTEs
)
SELECT ... FROM cte2;
```

---

### CTE vs Subquery

| | Subquery | CTE |
|---|---|---|
| Readability | Nested, harder to follow | Defined up top, easy to read |
| Reusability | Must repeat if used twice | Write once, use anywhere below |
| Debugging | Hard to isolate | Test each CTE separately |
| Performance | Same | Same |
| Use when | Simple one-off filter | Complex or reused logic |

---

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes

| Mistake | Fix |
|---|---|
| Forgetting ELSE | Always add ELSE — otherwise unmatched rows return NULL |
| Forgetting END | Every CASE must close with END |
| No alias after END | Always write `END AS column_name` |
| Comma after last CTE | Only commas BETWEEN CTEs — not after the last one |
| Using CTE in WHERE | Can't filter by a CASE alias in WHERE — use CTE or subquery |

